My monk had an encounter with Vecna yesterday showing me some of my
misconceptions about monster vulnerability but I also learned about the
awesome striking power of a monk at grand-master level.

Here is how I had planned things.  Having genocided L, I knew that Vecna
would be the only lich in the chaotic quest.  So I hoped to get to him
while he still was meditating.  I would then read a blessed scroll of
earth to protect myself and shoot him down with +3 bullets.  Since I was 
on the verge of getting burdened with my blessed bag of holding, I left 
it behind with my pets (4 monadic devas, 1 movanic deva, 1 ki-rin, and a 
warhorse).  What I took along for preparation were the following items: 
I wore water walking boots in case he would cast a spell of "create 
pool" under my feet (seems to be a rare event, though), I had a potion 
of speed ready to remain very fast nonetheless, I had a ring of 
levitation (to avoid squeaky boards), a wand of teleportation (0:5), a 
blessed scroll of scare monsters, two blessed potions of 
invulnerability, a diluted blessed potion of full healing, an uncursed 
ring of conflict, in case things went bad and there were too many 
monsters summoned (that's why I left my pets behind - I wanted to avoid 
being killed by them), a +0 assault rifle, some 50 +3 bullets.  My AC 
was -33.  I had some more stuff (+7 unicorn horn) but that is 
unessential for what happened.

When I entered the chaotic quest, I noticed that I did not have a scroll 
of magic mapping, so I returned to get one, read it in the quest, saw a 
room that seemed appropriate for retreating, teleported there to make 
sure I knew it.  Then I proceeded fighting vampires, fire vampires, 
wraiths and zombies (apparently fire vampires will not burn your scrolls 
or boil your potions - I was enveloped in flames twice without negative 
side effects).  Anyway, this seems to have been the wrong approach to 
surprise Vecna.  Suddenly, he was beside me in a hallway, drinking a 
potion of gain level.  I teleported back into my chosen hall, he 
followed immediately and did some 20 Hp of damage (I had about 220).  I 
dropped the scroll of scare monster to buy a little time and read the 
scroll of earth.  Vecna was still beside me, but wandered off shortly 
after.  Without the scroll of scare monster, he would have stayed close, 
and I could have started hitting him, but he could have hit me, too, and 
I was too respectful of him for the time being, still thinking I had a 
much safer way to beat him.  (With hindsight, it would have been better 
to set up my fortress in a hallway, leaving only two places for Vecna 
next to me, keeping him in my line of fire all the time).  I then 
wielded my gun and shot at him.  Big surprise: "You shoot five bullets. 
The first bullet hits it.  The bullet does not seem to harm it..." And 
so on.  The last time I had killed him without problems with +4 bullets, 
but +3 did not seem enough.  (I checked in wizard mode afterwards that 
the enchantment of the rifle is unimportant, but that the bullets have 
to be +4 at least for Vecna and +3 for vampire lords, while +2 suffices 
for couatls.) I was sufficiently consternated to save the game and to 
start thinking (I had believed that +2 is sufficient for any kind of 
monster...).

I fired it up soon again.  My plan was to teleport to the magic portal, 
get out as soon as I could without Vecna following me, get to my bag of 
holding, take out my only scroll of enchant armour left, enchant the 
bullets some more, get back to my fortress and repeat.  To be safe for 
some time, I quaffed a potion of invulnerability (I had already quaffed 
the potion of speed).  Vecna followed me to the place next to the portal 
immediately.  He summoned a purple worm who blocked the portal and some 
other critter (a giant, I believe).  Since I could not go through the 
portal immediately, I decided to hit him.  But I had not unwielded my 
rifle and consequently did no damage on the first hit (something to 
avoid next time...).  He hit me heavily several times and even created 
severe wounds on my body, but nothing harmed me.  I hit back hard, 
hoping to make him move away.  The purple worm swallowed me twice, but 
never liked my taste.  After spitting me out the second time, he was no 
longer on the site of the portal.  I considered going through and let my 
pets deal with Vecna (some of them were level 25 and would have attacked 
him), but I did not like the idea of a long fight with many 
imponderabilities.  So I first checked Vecna's state with my 
stethoscope.  What, only 18 Hp left?  I invoked the chi strike technique 
and he was history, leaving his hand and the Key of Chaos.  I finished 
off the purple worm and the giant, and picked up the sought-for hand as 
well as the key.  Both blasted me but did not do any harm.

My question after this long story is:  Are there any spoilers telling 
which monster is vulnerable at what enchantment of a weapon?  I intend 
to try to shoot Demogorgon as well, and I would be vexed, if +4 bullets 
did not suffice for him...

Another question that poses itself, how are artifacts affected by these 
different levels of monster vulnerability?  I noticed that I have no 
problems hitting high-level monsters with the unenchanted Excalibur (but 
I do more damage with martial arts, it appears) but that one of my 
monadic devas cannot hurt them with Sunsword.  I figured that this is 
due to the fact that Excalibur does +10 extra damage to any monster even 
unenchanted, and that Sunsword did not do extra damage to the attacked 
Angel and thus would not be able to harm him (it should however work on 
vampire lords according to this reasoning).  Is this idea right, i.e., 
does the extra damage done by the artifact count the same way as an 
extra enchantment would?  If it is not right, how to determine the 
chances to harm a high-level monster with an unenchanted artifact? 
Would Magicbane be able to do damage to Vecna, for example?
